---
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import { AwsOrgScrolly } from "../components/AwsOrgScrolly";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const entry = await getEntry("blog", "aws-org-terraform");
if (!entry) throw new Error("blog/aws-org-terraform entry missing");

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title={entry.data.title} description={entry.data.description}>
  <main>
    <p class="post-back">
      <a href={`${base}/`}>&larr; all posts</a>
    </p>

    <section class="intro" data-pagefind-body>
      <h1>An AWS org bootstrap that doesn&rsquo;t trust itself</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">
        Principle of Least Privilege applied to AWS Organizations and infrastructure-as-code.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most &ldquo;set up an AWS org in Terraform&rdquo; posts end with
        <code>terraform apply</code> from a single profile that has admin
        everywhere. That works until it doesn&rsquo;t: some copy-pasta sneaks in
        or someone runs an apply with the wrong AWS profile active and now
        your IaC state-storage account silently grows an EKS cluster or an RDS instance.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even when a small team starts out, it&rsquo;s worth setting up a new AWS org with
        clear boundaries and least privilege to avoid surprises later.
        Retrofitting isolation  and least privilege can be a nightmare but if you start clean,
        each piece feels simple and easy on its own.
      </p>

      <p><em>Scroll to begin.</em></p>
    </section>

    <AwsOrgScrolly client:load />

    <section class="body">
      <h2>The pattern, in one diagram</h2>
      <p>
        Three AWS accounts, each with one job:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Account</th><th>AWS profile</th><th>Job</th><th>Used by</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Management</strong></td>
            <td><code>org</code></td>
            <td>The Org itself, OUs, IAM Identity Center, permission sets, group assignments.</td>
            <td><code>terraform/aws/org/main.tf</code> only.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>terraform</strong></td>
            <td><code>terraform</code></td>
            <td>Hosts the state bucket and the lock. Nothing else.</td>
            <td>Every workspace&rsquo;s <code>backend.tf</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>app-dev / app-prod / sandbox-*</strong></td>
            <td><code>app-dev</code> / <code>app-prod</code> / etc.</td>
            <td>Real workloads &mdash; EKS, RDS, IAM roles, the actual product.</td>
            <td>Each workspace&rsquo;s <code>main.tf</code> provider block.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        Two profiles per workspace. The <code>terraform</code> profile is
        narrow: read state, write state, acquire the lock. Nothing else.
        The workload profile is broad <em>within its target account</em>
        and powerless everywhere else. They never overlap on a single
        credential.
      </p>

      <h2>The chicken-and-egg list</h2>
      <p>
        A useful exercise: write down the things Terraform <em>cannot</em>
        do, before writing down anything Terraform <em>will</em> do.
        There are exactly four:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Sign up for the first AWS account (browser, credit card, email).</li>
        <li>
          Enable MFA on the root user, store the recovery codes somewhere
          you&rsquo;ll find again, and lock the root user away.
        </li>
        <li>
          Enable IAM Identity Center in the management account
          (one click, one time).
        </li>
        <li>
          Create the very first SSO user + an <code>AdministratorAccess</code>
          permission set so you have a non-root identity to log in as.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        After (4), the AWS CLI can do <code>aws sso login</code>, Terraform
        can read those credentials, and from then on every change is HCL
        and a pull request.
      </p>

      <h2>Narrowing the org-runner credential</h2>
      <p>
        The credential we just bootstrapped &mdash; an SSO user assigned
        to <code>AdministratorAccess</code> on the management account &mdash;
        is broader than we want it to be for daily org operations. It can
        do anything in management: create EKS clusters, RDS instances,
        IAM users, anything. We don&rsquo;t want anything in management
        except the org tree, Identity Center, and account assignments. So
        the very first <code>terraform apply</code> run by the bootstrap
        admin defines a <em>narrower</em> permission set called
        <code>OrgManagement</code>, then assigns the (small) org-mgmt group
        to it on the management account.
      </p>
      <p>
        The word &ldquo;narrow&rdquo; needs unpacking, because Identity
        Center has four moving parts and only one of them is changing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A <strong>user</strong> (you, with email + MFA) lives in the
          identity store.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Groups</strong> (<code>org-mgmt</code>,
          <code>break-glass</code>, &hellip;) bag users together.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Permission sets</strong> (<code>AdministratorAccess</code>,
          <code>OrgManagement</code>) are reusable IAM-policy blobs.
        </li>
        <li>
          An <strong>account assignment</strong> is the actual grant: a
          3-tuple of <em>(principal, permission set, target account)</em>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Narrowing the bootstrap admin doesn&rsquo;t delete or rebuild the
        user, and doesn&rsquo;t throw away the <code>AdministratorAccess</code>
        permset. It only edits the <em>account-assignments</em> table:
        a new row for <code>OrgManagement</code> on the management account,
        and (eventually) the removal of the row that grants
        <code>AdministratorAccess</code> on management to your everyday
        identity. Same human, same SSO login flow, less power.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the same pattern we&rsquo;ll apply to state access in a
        moment, just on the other surface. Replace a broad credential with
        a narrow one as soon as you have something to narrow it with.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# org/permsets.tf
resource "aws_ssoadmin_permission_set" "org_management" {
  name         = "OrgManagement"
  instance_arn = local.sso_instance_arn
}

resource "aws_ssoadmin_managed_policy_attachment" "org_organizations" {
  instance_arn       = local.sso_instance_arn
  permission_set_arn = aws_ssoadmin_permission_set.org_management.arn
  managed_policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AWSOrganizationsFullAccess"
}

resource "aws_ssoadmin_managed_policy_attachment" "org_sso" {
  instance_arn       = local.sso_instance_arn
  permission_set_arn = aws_ssoadmin_permission_set.org_management.arn
  managed_policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AWSSSOMasterAccountAdministrator"
}

resource "aws_ssoadmin_permission_set_inline_policy" "org_extras" {
  instance_arn       = local.sso_instance_arn
  permission_set_arn = aws_ssoadmin_permission_set.org_management.arn
  inline_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [{
      Effect   = "Allow",
      Action   = ["identitystore:*", "iam:CreateServiceLinkedRole"],
      Resource = "*",
    }],
  })
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Three policies stitched together: <code>AWSOrganizationsFullAccess</code>
        (the org tree, OUs, accounts, SCPs), <code>AWSSSOMasterAccountAdministrator</code>
        (Identity Center config, permission sets, account assignments),
        and a small inline policy for the identity-store API and the
        service-linked role Identity Center needs the first time you
        assign a permission set. Together they cover everything
        <code>terraform/aws/org/</code> needs to manage. <code>IAMFullAccess</code>
        was overshooting; we don&rsquo;t need to create arbitrary IAM users
        or roles in the management account.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the new permset created, the actual flip is just an
        <code>aws_ssoadmin_account_assignment</code>: principal =
        <code>org-mgmt</code> group, permission_set =
        <code>OrgManagement</code>, target = management account. Add
        yourself to the group, log out, log back in selecting
        <code>OrgManagement</code> instead of <code>AdministratorAccess</code>
        in the role picker. The <code>AdministratorAccess</code> permset
        still exists; we just stop assigning your everyday identity to
        it on management. We keep one tiny <code>break-glass</code> group
        with that assignment for emergencies. Everyone else&rsquo;s daily
        account-assignment list on management shrinks to
        <code>OrgManagement</code> only.
      </p>
      <p>
        Net effect: a typo that drops a <code>{`resource "aws_eks_cluster" "oops"`}</code>
        into <code>terraform/aws/org/main.tf</code> fails the apply at IAM
        instead of creating an EKS cluster in the management account. The
        mistake is contained at the credential, not at the &ldquo;carefully
        reviewed PR&rdquo; layer.
      </p>
      <p>
        One footnote on the chronology: this very first apply runs with
        <em>local</em> state on your laptop, because we don&rsquo;t have an
        S3 backend yet &mdash; the <code>terraform</code> account that holds
        it is the next step. The local <code>terraform.tfstate</code> file
        sticks around for one or two more applies, then gets migrated to
        S3 with <code>terraform init -migrate-state</code>. After that, no
        <code>.tfstate</code> ever sits on a laptop again.
      </p>

      <h2>The dedicated <code>terraform</code> account</h2>
      <p>
        This is the unusual part of the setup, so it&rsquo;s worth making
        the case for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most posts on this topic put the state bucket either (a) in the
        management account, or (b) in the workload account being managed
        (one bucket per environment). Both are reasonable. The trade-offs:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>State in management:</strong> centralized, easy to back
          up, easy to grant access to. But the management account is also
          where you keep the org tree, SSO, billing &mdash; very high-value,
          frequently-touched. Mixing the &ldquo;keep this safe&rdquo; account
          with the &ldquo;everyone&rsquo;s state lives here&rdquo; account
          fights the principle that you don&rsquo;t want lots of identities
          getting tokens for the management account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>State per workload account:</strong> blast-radius nice for
          the workload itself &mdash; if you nuke <code>app-dev</code>,
          you nuke its state along with it, and that&rsquo;s arguably fine.
          But cross-environment terraform (e.g. an org-wide module that
          touches three accounts at once) becomes awkward, and you end up
          juggling N state backends.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>State in a dedicated account:</strong> centralized like
          option (a), but isolated from the org/SSO/billing surface area.
          Every workspace points at the same bucket; the access policy is
          one IAM resource; the audit trail is one CloudTrail. Costs you
          one extra AWS account (free) and one provisioning step.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The dedicated-account option is the one we picked. The whole pattern
        falls out of one IAM policy.
      </p>

      <h3>The fifteen-line policy that makes it work</h3>
      <p>
        Inside the <code>terraform</code> account, we define exactly one
        custom IAM policy:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# terraform/aws/terraform/main.tf — the policy that locks the box

resource "aws_iam_policy" "terraform_state_access" {
  name        = "TerraformStateAccess"
  description = "Read/write Terraform state. Nothing else."
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect   = "Allow",
        Action   = "s3:ListBucket",
        Resource = aws_s3_bucket.terraform_state.arn,
      },
      {
        Effect   = "Allow",
        Action   = ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject", "s3:DeleteObject"],
        Resource = "\${aws_s3_bucket.terraform_state.arn}/*",
      },
    ],
  })
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Four S3 actions, one bucket. Anyone with this policy can read, write,
        and lock state &mdash; and nothing else, anywhere in the
        <code>terraform</code> account. If a misconfigured Terraform module
        ever tries to <code>{`resource "aws_s3_bucket" "oops" { provider = aws.terraform_account }`}</code>,
        the API call fails at the IAM layer. The mistake is contained at
        the credential, not at the &ldquo;carefully reviewed PR&rdquo;
        layer.
      </p>
      <p>
        We expose the policy as an Identity Center permission set called
        <code>TerraformStateAccess</code>, and assign it to the engineering
        group on the <code>terraform</code> account &mdash; and only the
        <code>terraform</code> account.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`resource "aws_ssoadmin_permission_set" "terraform_state_access" {
  name         = "TerraformStateAccess"
  instance_arn = local.sso_instance_arn
}

resource "aws_ssoadmin_customer_managed_policy_attachment" "terraform_state_access" {
  instance_arn       = aws_ssoadmin_permission_set.terraform_state_access.instance_arn
  permission_set_arn = aws_ssoadmin_permission_set.terraform_state_access.arn
  customer_managed_policy_reference {
    name = "TerraformStateAccess"
    path = "/"
  }
}

resource "aws_ssoadmin_account_assignment" "terraform_engineering_state_access" {
  instance_arn       = local.sso_instance_arn
  permission_set_arn = aws_ssoadmin_permission_set.terraform_state_access.arn

  principal_id   = aws_identitystore_group.groups["engineering"].group_id
  principal_type = "GROUP"

  target_id   = aws_organizations_account.terraform.id
  target_type = "AWS_ACCOUNT"
}`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Aside: Terraform 1.11 killed the DynamoDB lock table</h2>
      <p>
        For most of Terraform&rsquo;s life, the canonical S3 backend
        recipe was &ldquo;S3 bucket for state, DynamoDB table for locking,
        coordinate them via <code>dynamodb_table</code> in the backend
        block.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s two AWS resources, two IAM policies, and
        a non-trivial bit of mental model.
      </p>
      <p>
        Terraform 1.11 (released Feb 2025) added native S3 lockfile support.
        The lock now lives as a small <code>.tflock</code> object next to
        the state file; the same four S3 actions cover it. The DynamoDB
        table is no longer required and the <code>dynamodb_table</code>
        argument is deprecated.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# the modern shape (TF >= 1.11)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket       = "dbnl-tfstate"
    key          = "app-dev"
    profile      = "terraform"
    region       = "us-east-1"
    use_lockfile = true        # was: dynamodb_table = "tfstate_lock"
    encrypt      = true
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        For our pattern, this also means the <em>second</em> custom IAM
        policy (<code>TerraformStateLockAccess</code>, four DynamoDB
        actions on one table) becomes redundant. Drop it. The
        <code>TerraformStateAccess</code> S3 policy alone now covers
        both state and lock.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you&rsquo;re migrating an existing setup, you can leave the
        DynamoDB table in place during the transition &mdash; Terraform
        accepts both <code>use_lockfile</code> and <code>dynamodb_table</code>
        simultaneously while you wait for every workspace to re-init.
      </p>

      <h2>The dual-profile split inside every workspace</h2>
      <p>
        With the <code>terraform</code> account and its narrow permission
        set in place, every workspace under <code>terraform/aws/</code>
        gets the same shape: two files, two profiles.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>backend.tf</code> uses the <code>terraform</code> profile.
        Its job is to read and write the state file. It cannot do anything
        else:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# terraform/aws/app/dev/backend.tf
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket       = "dbnl-tfstate"
    key          = "app-dev"
    profile      = "terraform"   # TerraformStateAccess only
    region       = "us-east-1"
    use_lockfile = true
    encrypt      = true
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>main.tf</code> uses the workload profile &mdash;
        <code>app-dev</code>, <code>app-prod</code>, <code>sandbox-dan</code>,
        whatever the workspace is for. Its job is to provision real
        resources. It cannot touch state:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# terraform/aws/app/dev/main.tf
provider "aws" {
  profile = "app-dev"   # admin in app-dev only
  region  = "us-east-1"
}

# ... resources ...`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The two profiles intentionally do not share a single permission.
        There is no terraform run that has both &ldquo;create EKS
        clusters&rdquo; and &ldquo;modify state&rdquo; on the same
        credential. Two profiles, two failure modes, no
        cross-contamination.
      </p>

      <h3>What this looks like in <code>~/.aws/config</code></h3>
      <p>
        After <code>aws configure sso</code> on a fresh laptop, you end
        up with three profiles backed by the Identity Center session:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`[sso-session sso]
sso_start_url  = https://distributional.awsapps.com/start
sso_region     = us-east-1
sso_registration_scopes = sso:account:access

[profile terraform]
sso_session    = sso
sso_account_id = 111111111111   # the terraform account
sso_role_name  = TerraformStateAccess
region         = us-east-1

[profile app-dev]
sso_session    = sso
sso_account_id = 222222222222   # the app-dev account
sso_role_name  = AdministratorAccess
region         = us-east-1

[profile org]
sso_session    = sso
sso_account_id = 333333333333   # the management account
sso_role_name  = AdministratorAccess
region         = us-east-1`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        One <code>aws sso login --sso-session sso</code> hydrates all
        three. <code>terraform init</code> picks up the
        <code>terraform</code> profile from the backend block;
        <code>terraform plan</code> picks up the workload profile from
        the provider block. The same human can do both, but each invocation
        only ever has the credentials it needs for the thing it&rsquo;s
        about to do.
      </p>

      <h2>The <code>org/</code> module: where the rest of the org lives</h2>
      <p>
        With the trust pattern established, the rest is mostly plumbing.
        The <code>org/</code> Terraform workspace, which uses the
        <code>org</code> profile (admin in management), defines:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The <code>aws_organizations_organization</code> resource itself.</li>
        <li>OUs (<code>app</code>, <code>sandbox</code>, <code>monitoring</code>, &hellip;).</li>
        <li>Member accounts under each OU.</li>
        <li>Identity Center users and groups.</li>
        <li>Permission sets (<code>AdministratorAccess</code>, <code>DevAccess</code>, <code>ReadOnlyAccess</code>, <code>TerraformStateAccess</code>, &hellip;).</li>
        <li>Account assignments &mdash; the join that says &ldquo;this group gets this permission set on this account.&rdquo;</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Pieces of it, abridged:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`resource "aws_organizations_organization" "org" {
  feature_set                   = "ALL"
  enabled_policy_types          = ["SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY"]
  aws_service_access_principals = ["sso.amazonaws.com"]
}

# OUs
resource "aws_organizations_organizational_unit" "app" {
  name      = "app"
  parent_id = aws_organizations_organization.org.roots[0].id
}

# Accounts. The lifecycle.ignore_changes is non-negotiable — without it,
# drift in any of these fields would force destroy/recreate, which closes
# the account.
resource "aws_organizations_account" "app_dev" {
  name      = "app-dev"
  email     = "aws+app-dev@acme.dev"
  parent_id = aws_organizations_organizational_unit.app.id
  role_name = "OrganizationAccountAccessRole"

  lifecycle { ignore_changes = [name, email, role_name] }
}

# SSO group + admin assignment on the workload account
resource "aws_identitystore_group" "engineering" {
  identity_store_id = local.identity_store_id
  display_name      = "Engineering"
}

resource "aws_ssoadmin_account_assignment" "app_dev_engineering_admin" {
  instance_arn       = local.sso_instance_arn
  permission_set_arn = aws_ssoadmin_permission_set.admin.arn
  principal_id       = aws_identitystore_group.engineering.group_id
  principal_type     = "GROUP"
  target_id          = aws_organizations_account.app_dev.id
  target_type        = "AWS_ACCOUNT"
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        If you already created the org by hand &mdash; and almost everyone
        does, since you needed it to enable Identity Center &mdash; the
        modern declarative <code>import {}</code> block (TF 1.5+) brings
        it under management without surprise:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`import {
  to = aws_organizations_organization.org
  id = "o-XXXXXXXX"
}

import {
  to = aws_organizations_account.management
  id = "111111111111"
}`}</code></pre>

      <h2>What we&rsquo;d add: SCPs</h2>
      <p>
        Service Control Policies are deny-fences attached to OUs. They
        cap what any IAM identity in that subtree can do, regardless of
        how permissive the IAM policy attached to that identity is.
      </p>
      <p>
        Honesty: our org doesn&rsquo;t have any SCPs attached yet. The
        org resource has <code>enabled_policy_types = ["SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY"]</code>
        so the capability is on, but no actual policies are bound. That&rsquo;s
        a gap, and one of the next things on the list. The starter set
        most teams want:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>deny-leave-organization:</strong> nobody, not even a workload-account admin, can detach.</li>
        <li><strong>deny-disable-cloudtrail:</strong> the audit trail is non-negotiable.</li>
        <li><strong>deny-region-allowlist:</strong> refuse anything outside the regions you actually deploy to. Stops accidental Mumbai sprawl.</li>
        <li><strong>deny-root-user-actions:</strong> deny <em>every</em> action where the principal is the root user. Forces SSO.</li>
      </ul>
      <pre><code>{`# Region allowlist — refuse any non-allowlisted region for non-global services.
data "aws_iam_policy_document" "region_allowlist" {
  statement {
    effect = "Deny"
    not_actions = [
      "iam:*", "organizations:*", "sts:*",
      "cloudfront:*", "route53:*", "support:*",  # global services
    ]
    resources = ["*"]
    condition {
      test     = "StringNotEquals"
      variable = "aws:RequestedRegion"
      values   = ["us-east-1", "us-west-2"]
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_organizations_policy" "region_allowlist" {
  name    = "region-allowlist"
  type    = "SERVICE_CONTROL_POLICY"
  content = data.aws_iam_policy_document.region_allowlist.json
}

resource "aws_organizations_policy_attachment" "region_allowlist_app" {
  policy_id = aws_organizations_policy.region_allowlist.id
  target_id = aws_organizations_organizational_unit.app.id
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Test SCPs in a sandbox OU first. They take effect <em>immediately</em>
        on attach, and a too-broad deny can lock you out of an account
        cleanly &mdash; you&rsquo;ll need to detach from the management
        account to recover. The management account itself is exempt from
        custom SCPs by default.
      </p>

      <h2>What I&rsquo;d skip on a first pass</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Control Tower.</strong> If you&rsquo;re a small team
          starting from scratch and want to <em>understand</em> what&rsquo;s
          happening, skip Control Tower for now. It paves over a lot of
          useful learning and adds an opinionated baseline that&rsquo;s
          painful to opt out of later. Reach for it (or AFT) once you have
          opinions about what you actually want.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>An external IdP (Okta, Google Workspace, Entra).</strong>
          Identity Center&rsquo;s built-in directory is fine for the first
          few months. Switching to an external IdP later is a configuration
          change in one place, not a rewrite.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A Terraform IAM user with long-lived keys.</strong>
          Don&rsquo;t. Use <code>aws sso login</code> and short-lived
          credentials from day one. Your future self will thank you the
          first time something gets leaked.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Footguns I&rsquo;ve hit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Account email reuse.</strong> AWS rejects creation
          even if the previous account was closed two years ago. Use
          plus-addressing on a domain you control.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Account closure via Terraform.</strong> There is no
          clean <code>terraform destroy</code> for an account. The
          <code>aws_organizations_account</code> resource can &ldquo;remove&rdquo;
          it from the org, but the account itself lingers. Closing it is
          a manual root-user action with a 90-day grace period.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Forgetting <code>lifecycle.ignore_changes</code>.</strong>
          AWS rejects in-place updates to <code>name</code>, <code>email</code>,
          and <code>role_name</code> on member accounts. Without the lifecycle
          rule, drift on those fields shows as a destroy/recreate plan, which
          is terrifying &mdash; recreate means closing the account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Moving an account between OUs.</strong> Works, but
          double-check the plan &mdash; you want an in-place update of
          <code>parent_id</code>, not a destroy/recreate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>SSO assignment is rate-limited.</strong> The
          <code>aws_ssoadmin_account_assignment</code> resource is slow.
          On a big team with many accounts, expect <code>terraform apply</code>
          in <code>org/</code> to take 5&ndash;15 minutes.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="outro">
      <h2>What this gets you</h2>
      <p>
        Your Git history is the audit log for the entire organization
        structure. Every new account is a PR. Every SCP change is a PR.
        Adding a new engineer is a PR that adds them to a group; their
        access in every account changes the next time they re-run
        <code>aws sso login</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        And, more importantly: the day someone runs the wrong
        <code>terraform apply</code> from the wrong directory, the
        explosion is bounded. The credential they were using either had
        permission to write state but not to create resources, or had
        permission to create resources in <em>one</em> account but not
        anywhere else. The org is paranoid on the user&rsquo;s behalf.
      </p>
    </section>

